WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.968
-Wow.
You really have all the answers

00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:04.300
to my life figured out.
-Yeah, well, we're friends.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.667
Obviously, I have
well-thought-out critiques

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:11.000
of every choice you've ever
made, and all the fixes.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.100
So, if you end up going on this
Manhattan adventure

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.667
and find yourself a hottie,
might I suggest a manicure.

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:21.501
Maybe get waxed.
You know, spruce it up a bit.

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:23.300
-Waxed?
Oh, wa--

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.868
Oh, waxed.
-Waxed.

00:00:25.868 --> 00:00:28.334
-Oh, well that's just
not gonna happen.

00:00:28.334 --> 00:00:29.767
[ Laughter ]
-Welcome back to "Late Night"!

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:31.267
We're here
with Reese Witherspoon.

00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:33.467
[ Cheers and applause ]
That was a clip from

00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:36.901
"Your Place or Mine"
with the fantastic Tig Notaro,

00:00:36.901 --> 00:00:38.033
who plays your best friend.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:39.634
Tig Notaro is awesome.

00:00:39.634 --> 00:00:41.467
She's also
in "Morning Show" now.

00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:42.701
-Oh, wonderful.
Look at that.

00:00:42.701 --> 00:00:44.000
-Yeah.
Because I'm now apparently

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.567
not going anywhere
without Tig Notaro.

00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:46.767
-I don't blame you.

00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:47.934
She's a wonderful person
to write with.

00:00:47.934 --> 00:00:49.534
-She's so funny.
I've also been on her podcast.

00:00:49.534 --> 00:00:51.868
It's really funny.
-You know, you were talking

00:00:51.868 --> 00:00:53.234
about people watching movies
at home,

00:00:53.234 --> 00:00:55.000
and I think there is a great
comfort to doing that.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.334
And there's also a great comfort
in rom-coms

00:00:57.334 --> 00:00:59.701
that I sometimes
think people underestimate.

00:00:59.701 --> 00:01:01.734
And they must be really fun
to make

00:01:01.734 --> 00:01:03.367
because people
enjoy watching them so much.

00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:05.767
-Yeah, I think so.
It's also just not --

00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:08.467
It's not like taking a test.
-Yeah.

00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:09.901
-Some movies really feel like,

00:01:09.901 --> 00:01:11.200
"What was I supposed
to get out of that?"

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.601
-Right.
-"I just feel bad," you know?

00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:16.367
Or, "Oh, I'm supposed
to feel embarrassed

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:17.968
that I don't know about it."
I don't know.

00:01:17.968 --> 00:01:20.467
Anyway, this is just one of
those really easy, light,

00:01:20.467 --> 00:01:23.000
classic romantic comedies
with Ashton Kutcher.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.100
It's really fun and funny.
-Another person who's been

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.100
doing this for a long time,
but is it true,

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:28.534
you've never worked
with Ashton before?

00:01:28.534 --> 00:01:29.801
-Yeah, we never
worked together before.

00:01:29.801 --> 00:01:32.300
-That's amazing.
-And it was the perfect script.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.968
The woman, Aline Brosh McKenna,
who wrote it and directed it,

00:01:34.968 --> 00:01:37.934
she wrote "Devil Wears Prada"
and "27 Dresses"

00:01:37.934 --> 00:01:39.667
and "Crazy Ex-Girlfriend,"
so she's just --

00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:42.400
she's got a really funny tone
to her writing,

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.033
but she also understands
a lot about

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:49.167
just the idea
of what a modern romance is.

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:52.334
Like, it can't be corny
or not modern, right?

00:01:52.334 --> 00:01:54.434
So it's all about
a love letter to a single mom,

00:01:54.434 --> 00:01:56.367
that my character's a single mom
looking for love

00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:57.567
and second chances,

00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:00.334
and she finds it
in the most unlikely place.

00:02:00.334 --> 00:02:01.601
[ Laughter ]

00:02:01.601 --> 00:02:04.033
-Don't give too much away.
-New York City.

00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:06.501
-New York City?
This is the first rom-com

00:02:06.501 --> 00:02:07.634
that took place in New York.
Is that true?

00:02:07.634 --> 00:02:08.834
-It is.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:08.834 --> 00:02:11.033
-Wow. Somebody finally said,
"You know what?

00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:12.534
This would be a good place."
-Finally.

00:02:12.534 --> 00:02:15.667
Yes, with beautiful locations,
and the weather's always good.

00:02:15.667 --> 00:02:17.667
-Oh, good!
-Yes, like it is in New York.

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:20.000
-Yeah. Maybe fall.
Maybe some scenes in the fall?

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.801
-It's always in the fall.
-That's good, yeah.

00:02:21.801 --> 00:02:23.501
-Yeah.
-No one has shot

00:02:23.501 --> 00:02:26.634
a rom-com today.
Nobody did a February

00:02:26.634 --> 00:02:29.234
whatever -- yeah.
-Like blistering, blowing wind.

00:02:29.234 --> 00:02:31.801
No, it's not that.
-Is it true that --

00:02:31.801 --> 00:02:33.434
'cause I imagine this
has happened over the years.

00:02:33.434 --> 00:02:38.901
You've been on flights where
your film is the in-flight film.

00:02:38.901 --> 00:02:40.133
-Yes.
-For everybody.

00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:42.334
-Yes. Okay.
Back when they used to have

00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:44.334
one movie that played
on an airplane --

00:02:44.334 --> 00:02:45.467
this is actually a funny story.

00:02:45.467 --> 00:02:47.667
I was traveling --
And it was a long flight.

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:51.534
It was like a five-hour flight,
and I walk in, and they're like,

00:02:51.534 --> 00:02:53.701
"And the movie today is gonna be
'How Do You Know,'

00:02:53.701 --> 00:02:55.534
starring Reese Witherspoon,"
and I was like, "Oh, my God.

00:02:55.534 --> 00:02:56.801
I have nowhere to hide.
Oh, my God.

00:02:56.801 --> 00:02:58.033
I have nowhere to hide.
Oh, my God.

00:02:58.033 --> 00:03:00.167
I have nowhere to hide.
This is so embarrassing."

00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:02.367
And my kids are there too, and
they're also like, "Oh, my God.

00:03:02.367 --> 00:03:04.367
Our mom is so embarrassing."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.367 --> 00:03:07.167
And then, probably maybe
10 minutes into

00:03:07.167 --> 00:03:11.601
the in-flight movie, it cut out,
and the clip broke.

00:03:11.601 --> 00:03:13.701
The tape broke or something.
And I was like --

00:03:13.701 --> 00:03:15.000
[ Whispers ]
"Oh, my God. Oh, my God."

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.267
Everybody started looking at me
like, "Oh, my God.

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:18.734
Reese Witherspoon..."
And so I went to the front

00:03:18.734 --> 00:03:20.734
of the plane,
and I got on the microphone.

00:03:20.734 --> 00:03:23.133
I said to the flight attendant,
"Can I talk in the microphone?"

00:03:23.133 --> 00:03:25.100
I was like, "Hi, guys. I don't
think the movie's coming back,

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.834
so I'm just gonna walk you,
beat by beat..."

00:03:26.834 --> 00:03:28.567
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.567 --> 00:03:30.067
"...what happened.
So first of all,

00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:32.167
I'm going out with Owen Wilson.
It's not going well."

00:03:32.167 --> 00:03:33.801
[ Laughter ]
"And he's a professional

00:03:33.801 --> 00:03:35.801
baseball player, and you know
that's gonna go south.

00:03:35.801 --> 00:03:38.467
So I have this crazy great date
with Paul Rudd,

00:03:38.467 --> 00:03:41.200
but -- but -- his dad is asking
him to go to jail for him

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:42.567
because he's committed a crime.

00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:44.300
Guys, I don't know
what's gonna happen.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.801
Am I gonna go with Owen Wilson?
Am I gonna go with Paul Rudd?

00:03:46.801 --> 00:03:49.868
What's gonna happen?"
And they were like, "Yes!"

00:03:49.868 --> 00:03:51.234
-Oh, my God.
-It was really fun.

00:03:51.234 --> 00:03:53.400
-I just like that those people,
the rest of the year,

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.567
were telling people,
"You got to fly United

00:03:55.567 --> 00:03:58.067
because the movie
never finishes,

00:03:58.067 --> 00:04:00.834
but they have the star there."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.834 --> 00:04:02.234
"And they will
talk you through it."

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:05.067
-It was actually so fun
and funny to, like,

00:04:05.067 --> 00:04:07.167
get to tell people
the entire plot of my movie

00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:09.534
in like three minutes.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:09.534 --> 00:04:10.934
-What a delight
to have you back.

00:04:10.934 --> 00:04:12.434
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:04:12.434 --> 00:04:16.067
Congratulations on
"Morning Show," your new movie.

00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:17.467
You guys,
there's nobody like her.

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:18.801
Reese Witherspoon.

00:04:18.801 --> 00:04:21.234
"Your Place or Mine" premieres
on Netflix February 10th.

00:04:21.234 --> 00:04:23.133
We'll be right back
with Paula Pell.

